    {
      "filename": "Amazon Basin (MODIS).jpg",
      "title": "File:Amazon Basin (MODIS).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Amazon_Basin_%28MODIS%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amazon_Basin_(MODIS).jpg",
      "width": 3958,
      "height": 2827,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "MODIS Land Rapid Response Team, NASA GSFC",
      "description": "On September 27, 2023, the Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) on NASA’s Terra satellite acquired a true-color image of a nearly cloud-free day over the Amazon Basin.\nCentered on the state of Amazonas, Brazil, the image captures the Amazon River and its many tributaries winding through luxuriant growth of rainforest. The Amazon River appears muddy in many areas, where it carries sediment from the surrounding lands, including as far as the mountains of Peru, where the earliest tributaries rise.\n\nThe clear skies and relatively narrow width of the rivers are clues that show that this image was acquired well into the dry season, which runs from June to November. The driest month in the Amazon Basin is August, with an average rainfall is about 2 inches (5 cm). In contrast, rainfall from May – December is often more than 8 inches (20 cm) per month, with a total annual rainfall averaging between 6 – 10 feet (1.3 to 3 meters).",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 27 September 2023",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "amazon-basin-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "6z8f",
      "mediumPath": "_m/6/z/8/f/amazon-basin-wp/amazon-basin-modis-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/6/z/8/f/amazon-basin-wp/amazon-basin-modis-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Glow bug.jpg",
      "title": "File:Glow bug.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/Glow_bug.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glow_bug.jpg",
      "width": 5184,
      "height": 3456,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC0",
      "licenseUrl": "http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en",
      "artist": "LauraMaisväli",
      "description": "A bioluminescent bug. We found this bug during a night expedition in the Amazon rainforest, while looking for bioluminescence.  It had a bright blue glow to it. It is remarkable, because blue bioluminescence is extremely rare in land organisms, only found in one other species, in South America. The bug was killed by a little spider, as we tried to capture it. The bug might be a whole new species, yet unknown to science. Night expedition was a part of a volunteering programme at IPBio, (Instituto de pesquisas da biodiversidade).",
      "dateCreated": "2023-10-03 01:39:06",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
